### 1. Arthur Evans, “The ring of Nestor” (1925)

- **Title:** “The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia”
- **URL:** https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/evans1925 (main entry page; 
full PDF via “normal, 49 MB” link)
- **Author / Institution:** Arthur J. Evans; digitized by Universitätsbibliothek
Heidelberg (Heidelberg University Library)
- **Date:** 1925; digitized online since 17 June 2008
- **Source type:** Academic monograph / archaeological study (primary scholarly 
source)

**Query that triggered it:** `"Ring of Nestor" gold ring Pylos Mycenaean 
description iconography` and `"The ring of Nestor" Evans 1925 gold signet ring 
description`

**Relevance to goal:**
- This is the foundational publication on the Ring of Nestor, providing detailed
description, drawings, and interpretive discussion of its iconography. It is 
essential for physical description and for the original scholarly interpretation
of the scenes.

**Content summary (focused on the ring):**
- Evans presents the “Ring of Nestor” as a large gold signet ring attributed 
stylistically to the Minoan world but said to have been found at Thisbê (ancient
Thisbê, Boeotia) in central Greece. He connects it with the legendary Nestor, 
but his attribution is interpretive and not based on inscriptional evidence.[1]
- The ring is described as a **gold signet ring** with an elongated **elliptical
bezel** engraved in **intaglio** with a complex mytho‑ritual scene. Evans 
reproduces the bezel in collotype plates and a detailed line drawing (by Émile 
Gilliéron fils). The text of the monograph gives measurements (length of bezel, 
approximate thickness and size of hoop) and emphasizes the unusually large 
dimensions and high craftsmanship compared to typical Minoan and Mycenaean 
rings.[1]
- Evans discusses the **craftsmanship**: he stresses the fineness of the gold 
work, the precise cutting of figures in intaglio, and the elaborate composition 
in several registers. The quality of the engraving is taken as evidence for a 
Cretan (Minoan) workshop.[1]
- **Iconography (as described and interpreted by Evans):**
  - The bezel shows a **multi‑part ritual or funerary scene**. Evans’ 
description identifies:
    - A female figure on a **boat** (interpreted as a soul or divinity traveling
in the “after‑world”).
    - **Altars or platforms**, **sacred trees**, and **baetyls** (sacred stones)
arranged in a way suggesting a ceremonial landscape.
    - Multiple **female figures** engaged in ritual actions; Evans interprets 
them as priestesses or goddesses attending a soul’s journey, connecting the 
imagery to Minoan beliefs about the afterlife.[1]
  - He reads the composition as a “glimpse into the Minoan after‑world”, 
suggesting the ring was created as part of elite funerary equipment whose 
imagery reflects notions of the soul’s voyage and cultic rites.[1]
- Evans situates the Ring of Nestor among other **gold signet rings and bead 
seals** from the same group of finds from Thisbê and compares its imagery to 
other Minoan religious scenes, emphasizing continuities in symbolism such as 
sacred trees, altars, and cult stones.[1]
- The monograph does not itself offer a modern assessment of authenticity but 
presents the ring as genuine Minoan‑style work found in Greece.

*(Note: for exact dimensional data you will need to extract the numerals from 
the PDF pages in Part I, where Evans gives the measurements of the rings and 
plates depicting the Ring of Nestor.)*

