### Notes on physical description gap

The search results strongly indicate that the most detailed physical description
and measurements of the Ring of Nestor are in Evans’s 1925 monograph.[1] The 
digitized volume’s main entry page outlines its contents but does not itself 
list the ring’s dimensions; you will need to open the PDF and consult:

- Part I (“Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, 
Boeotia”) for technical description of each ring.
- Plates I–V (especially those devoted to the Ring of Nestor) for visual 
documentation and any plate captions specifying lengths, widths, and workmanship
details.

From other scholarly snippets (Cambridge chapter and iconography papers), we can
reliably infer certain iconographic components—female figure on boat, altars, 
sacred trees, baetyls—but physical data must be taken directly from Evans or 
another technical catalogue if available.

