      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Arthur J. Evans' 1925 monograph introduces and 
analyzes the gold signet ring he calls the 'Ring of Nestor', presented as part 
of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals reportedly found at
ancient Thisbê in Boeotia. Evans describes the ring as an unusually large gold 
signet with an elongated elliptical bezel engraved in intaglio, emphasizing its 
high-quality craftsmanship and considering its style to be Minoan, likely 
produced in a Cretan workshop. He provides technical measurements and 
reproductions (collotype plates and a detailed line drawing) to document the 
ring's physical form and engraved scene. The iconographic analysis interprets 
the bezel scene as a complex ritual or funerary composition comprising multiple 
female figures, a boat carrying a female figure, altars or platforms, sacred 
trees, and baetyls (cult stones), arranged to form a ceremonial landscape. Evans
proposes that the imagery reflects Minoan beliefs about the afterworld and the 
soul's journey and connects the ring conceptually with the legendary Nestor, 
though without epigraphic confirmation. He situates the ring within a wider 
group of gold signet rings and bead seals from the same find and compares its 
iconography with other Minoan religious scenes featuring sacred trees, altars, 
and cult stones.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
