at the Palace of Nestor in Pylos, dating to Late Helladic IIA and containing 
rich grave goods including four gold signet rings engraved with Minoan ritual 
scenes. The authors argue that many artifacts in the tomb were manufactured in 
the Minoan world and use the rings' iconography to explore connections between 
Cretan and Mycenaean elite culture. In their comparative discussion, they 
reference the 'Ring of Nestor' as a well-known example of a mainland-found gold 
signet ring decorated with Minoan-style ceremonial imagery. The article notes 
recurring motifs in this corpus, such as female figures performing rituals, 
altars, sacred trees, baetyls, animals, and boats, and emphasizes that these 
scenes appear in Mycenaean funerary contexts while drawing on Minoan religious 
imagery. The Ring of Nestor is treated as an influential comparator for 
interpreting the new rings' iconography and for understanding how Mycenaean 
elites adopted Minoan ritual scenes on prestigious gold signet rings.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
