## 1. Arthur Evans, “‘The Ring of Nestor’: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World
and a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, 
Boeotia” (1925)

- **Title**: ‘The Ring of Nestor’: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a 
Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia  
- **URL** (digitized monograph / offprint):  
  - Heidelberg University Library (full PDF and plates):  
    https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/evans1925  
  - Cambridge / Journal of Hellenic Studies PDF (article version):  
    https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/5E46
5BD1031E9251C962E138498E6CFA/S0075426900084196a.pdf/ring_of_nestor_a_glimpse_int
o_the_minoan_afterworld_and_a_sepulchral_treasure_of_gold_signetrings_and_beadse
als_from_thisbe_boeotia.pdf  
- **Author / Publisher**: Arthur J. Evans; originally in *The Journal of 
Hellenic Studies*; digitized by Heidelberg University Library and Cambridge 
University Press.  
- **Date**: 1925 (article); online publication of the Cambridge PDF 23 December 
2013.  
- **Source type**: Peer‑reviewed journal article / scholarly monograph.  
- **Query that triggered it**:  
  - `"\"The Ring of Nestor\": A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a 
Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals Evans 1925"`

### Content (faithful summary)

- **Discovery context**:  
  - Evans reports a “royal treasure of gold rings and of bead-seals” discovered 
in 1915 in a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, close to 
the village of Dombrena, northwest of Kakosi on the acropolis height of 
Thisbê.[1]  
  - The find was made by a local peasant under wartime conditions, outside 
official archaeological supervision; Evans states he cannot give all details 
without breaking confidences but describes the general location and associated 
tombs.[1]  
  - He notes that W. A. Heurtley of the British School at Athens had been shown 
chamber tombs in the same area, with finds including a dagger, vases (including 
a late-type stirrup jar), bronze spearheads, a short sword, a bronze razor, and 
a small perforated iron double‑axe.[1]  
  - Evans links associated jewelry and glass-paste objects to early Late Minoan 
III B, and aligns the tomb group with Late Mycenaean chronology (broadly 
13th–12th century BC).  

- **Dating and cultural attribution**:  
  - Evans classifies the ring and associated treasure as Mycenaean, but strongly
emphasizes Minoan stylistic and religious elements. He situates the iconography 
within the Late Bronze Age “Minoan” religious milieu, using Cretan parallels for
weapons, jewelry, and glyptic motifs.[1]  
  - He uses comparative typological evidence (weapons, glass-paste, faience 
plaques, etc.) to place the assemblage late in the Mycenaean period, while the 
ring’s imagery is seen as reflecting an earlier, classical “Minoan” iconographic
repertoire.[1]  

- **Physical description and iconography**:  
  - Evans presents the “Ring of Nestor” as a large gold signet ring, part of a 
hoard of gold signet rings and bead-seals. Plates and figures (Tafel I–V) show 
detailed drawings and photographs (not reproduced here but available in the 
PDF).[1]  
  - He describes the ring as bearing a complex scene with:  
    - A sacred tree flanked by figures and ritual elements;  
    - Altars or baetyls (standing sacred stones);  
    - A female figure / goddess in a ritual context (he associates her with 
Minoan divinity);  
    - Animals and possibly a “watch‑dog” or “dragon” as guardian of the sacred 
tree.[2]  
  - Evans describes the composition as densely populated and calls it one of the
most elaborate pictorial scenes known in Aegean glyptic.[2]  

- **Interpretive framework (“Minoan after‑world”)**:  
  - Evans interprets the ring’s scene as a glimpse into a Minoan “after‑world”, 
a kind of eschatological or funerary landscape involving the soul’s journey and 
ritual contact with divinities.[2]  
  - He reads the sacred tree, altars/baetyls, and goddess as part of a cultic 
complex reflecting Minoan beliefs about death and the afterlife, with parallels 
to other Cretan gold signet rings and sealstones.[2]  
  - The “watch‑dog” or “dragon” is presented as a guardian figure connected with
the “world tree” and otherworldly realm.[2]  
  - The interpretation is explicitly speculative, but influential; later 
scholars negotiate with or react against Evans’s reading of a “Minoan 
after‑world.”  

- **Significance according to Evans**:  
  - Evans emphasizes:  
    - The ring as a key document for Minoan–Mycenaean religion and myth, 
particularly notions of the afterlife;  
    - Its importance as a masterpiece of Aegean glyptic art, comparable with the
most elaborate Cretan signet rings;  
    - Its value for understanding the spread of Minoan religious imagery into 
mainland Greece (Boeotia) via Mycenaean elites.[1][2]  

