## 4. Andreas G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the Quest for 
Authenticity” (2020)

- **Title**: “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity”  
- **Publication**: In F. Blakolmer (ed.), *Current Approaches and New 
Perspectives in Aegean Iconography*, Aegis 18, 2020, pp. 223–252.  
- **URL**:  
  - Academia-hosted preprint / PDF:  
    https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A_G_Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_
and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F_Blakolmer_ed_Current_Approaches_and_New_Pers
pectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_Aegis_18_2020_pp_223_252  
  - The broader volume (KU ScholarWorks PDF – full proceedings):  
    https://kuscholarworks.ku.edu/bitstreams/184d9df4-9373-4fb9-8d76-fea30082c29
6/download  
- **Author / Publisher**: Andreas G. Vlachopoulos; Aegis series (Université 
catholique de Louvain / Aegis).  
- **Date**: 2020.  
- **Source type**: Chapter in edited academic volume.  
- **Query that triggered it**:  
  - `"some observations on the iconography of the 'ring of nestor'"`  
  - initial Serper hit: “Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean 
Iconography”  

### Content (from abstract and “key takeaways”)

- **Scope**:  
  - The paper documents the history of research on the Ring of Nestor, focusing 
on the **authentication process** and misidentifications of Cretan–Mycenaean 
gold rings as forgeries.[5]  

- **Authenticity reassessment**:  
  - Vlachopoulos notes that earlier scholarship from the 1920s onward often 
regarded the Ring of Nestor (and the Ring of Minos) with suspicion due to the 
obscure circumstances of discovery and highly elaborate imagery.[5]  
  - He argues that this skepticism has been “completely reassessed in favour of 
their authenticity,” thanks to:  
    - New finds of comparable iconographic subjects (wall paintings, gold rings,
figurines, ivories) dated securely to the early Late Bronze Age (16th–15th 
centuries BC);  
    - More detailed study of the techniques and craftsmanship of these large 
gold signet rings.[5]  
  - The ring is explicitly dated to the “first centuries of the Late Bronze Age 
(16th–15th century BC)” on stylistic grounds.[5]  

- **Iconographic reinterpretation**:  
  - The “key pictorial unit” discussed is what Evans called the “watch‑dog” or 
“dragon,” a guardian of the “old tree of the world” whose branches host a 
densely populated scene.[5]  
  - Vlachopoulos re-identifies this motif as a **scorpion**, described as a 
major apotropaic (protective) symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography; this 
scorpion is said to “kill the ‘Minoan Dragon’ of the ring,” producing a new 
interpretation of the scene’s narrative and symbolism.[5]  
  - The reinterpretation shifts emphasis from a vague “dragon” guardian to a 
specific scorpion motif, tying the ring’s imagery more closely to known 
apotropaic symbols and mythological structures in Aegean art.  

- **Significance**:  
  - Strengthens the case that the ring’s complex iconography is rooted in 
genuine Aegean Bronze Age religious imagery, not a modern fabrication.  
  - Shows how evolving corpora and comparative material can overturn earlier 
doubts and recast the ring as a cornerstone of Aegean iconographic studies.  

