near Thisbê in Boeotia, close to the village of Dombrena on the acropolis height
of Thisbê.", "document_ref": "d1", "source_ref": "s1", "qualifiers": ["location:
Thisbê, Boeotia", "discovery_year: 1915"], "limitations": ["based on second-hand
account from local finder"], "confidence": "high"},
    {"local_ref": "st2", "text": "Evans states that associated finds from the 
chamber tombs near Thisbê included a dagger, Late Mycenaean vases such as a 
stirrup jar, bronze spearheads, a short sword, a bronze razor, and a small 
perforated iron double-axe.", "document_ref": "d1", "source_ref": "s1", 
"qualifiers": ["associated_grave_goods"], "limitations": [], "confidence": 
"high"},
    {"local_ref": "st3", "text": "Evans classifies the Ring of Nestor and its 
associated treasure as Mycenaean in date but emphasizes that their style and 
religious imagery show strong Minoan elements.", "document_ref": "d1", 
"source_ref": "s1", "qualifiers": ["cultural_attribution: Mycenaean with Minoan 
influence"], "limitations": [], "confidence": "high"},
    {"local_ref": "st4", "text": "Evans places the tomb group and the Ring of 
Nestor in the late Mycenaean period, broadly corresponding to the 13th–12th 
century BC, based on typological comparisons with weapons, jewelry, and 
glass-paste objects.", "document_ref": "d1", "source_ref": "s1", "qualifiers": 
["chronology: Late Mycenaean", "approx_date_range: 13th–12th century BC"], 
"limitations": ["chronology inferred from typology"], "confidence": "medium"},
    {"local_ref": "st5", "text": "Evans describes the Ring of Nestor as a large 
gold signet ring bearing a complex scene that includes a sacred tree, altars or 
baetyls, a female figure interpreted as a goddess, ritual elements, and an 
animal guardian he terms a watch-dog or dragon.", "document_ref": "d1", 
"source_ref": "s1", "qualifiers": ["object_type: gold signet ring"], 
"limitations": [], "confidence": "high"},
    {"local_ref": "st6", "text": "Evans interprets the scene on the Ring of 
Nestor as depicting a Minoan after-world, presenting it as an eschatological or 
funerary landscape involving the soul’s journey and ritual contact with 
divinities.", "document_ref": "d1", "source_ref": "s1", "qualifiers": 
["interpretation_type: religious_afterlife_scene"], "limitations": 
["interpretation is explicitly speculative"], "confidence": "medium"},
    {"local_ref": "st7", "text": "Evans argues that the Ring of Nestor is a key 
document for understanding Minoan–Mycenaean religion and myth, particularly 
beliefs about the afterlife, and that it provides evidence for the spread of 
Minoan religious imagery into mainland Greece.", "document_ref": "d1", 
"source_ref": "s1", "qualifiers": ["topic: religious_significance"], 
"limitations": [], "confidence": "high"},
    {"local_ref": "st8", "text": "The Heidelberg digitization shows that the 
Ring of Nestor is a large gold signet ring with a densely populated pictorial 
scene, corroborating Evans’s description of it as one of the most elaborate 
compositions in Aegean glyptic.", "document_ref": "d2", "source_ref": "s2", 
"qualifiers": ["object_type: gold signet ring"], "limitations": ["visual 
assessment based on plates"], "confidence": "high"},
    {"local_ref": "st9", "text": "Pini notes that the authenticity of the Ring 
of Nestor had previously been questioned primarily on iconographic grounds, with
some scholars arguing that its imagery seemed inconsistent with known Aegean 
Bronze Age art and might indicate a modern forgery.", "document_ref": "d3", 
"source_ref": "s3", "qualifiers": ["topic: authenticity_debate"], "limitations":
["based on abstract summary"], "confidence": "medium"},
    {"local_ref": "st10", "text": "Pini presents new technical observations on 
the Ring of Nestor, such as evidence from the ring’s manufacture and material, 
and concludes that these observations support its authenticity as an ancient 
artifact.", "document_ref": "d3", "source_ref": "s3", "qualifiers": ["topic: 
technical_authentication"], "limitations": ["details of tests not available in 
snippet"], "confidence": "medium"},
    {"local_ref": "st11", "text": "Eliopoulos states that the Ring of Nestor has
been central to debates about Aegean forgeries and authenticity.", 
"document_ref": "d4", "source_ref": "s4", "qualifiers": ["topic: 
authenticity_debate"], "limitations": [], "confidence": "medium"},
    {"local_ref": "st12", "text": "Eliopoulos analyses the ring’s sacred trees, 
incurved altars, baetyls, and guardian animals and argues that these motifs have
close parallels in securely dated Aegean Bronze Age imagery.", "document_ref": 
"d4", "source_ref": "s4", "qualifiers": ["interpretation_type: 
iconographic_comparison"], "limitations": ["based on article summary rather than
full text"], "confidence": "medium"},
    {"local_ref": "st13", "text": "Eliopoulos concludes that the iconographic 
parallels between the Ring of Nestor and other Aegean religious scenes support 
the ring’s authenticity and weaken earlier claims that its composition is too 
eclectic or fantastic to be ancient.", "document_ref": "d4", "source_ref": "s4",
"qualifiers": ["topic: iconographic_authentication"], "limitations": ["based on 
summarized argument"], "confidence": "medium"},
    {"local_ref": "st14", "text": "Eliopoulos maintains that iconography remains
an essential criterion for assessing the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor, 
even in light of technical analyses.", "document_ref": "d4", "source_ref": "s4",
"qualifiers": ["methodology: iconographic_approach"], "limitations": [], 
"confidence": "medium"},
    {"local_ref": "st15", "text": "Vlachopoulos reports that from the 1920s 
onward many scholars regarded the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos with 
suspicion because of their obscure discovery circumstances and highly elaborate 
imagery.", "document_ref": "d5", "source_ref": "s5", "qualifiers": ["topic: 
research_history"], "limitations": [], "confidence": "medium"},
    {"local_ref": "st16", "text": "Vlachopoulos argues that new discoveries of 
wall paintings, gold rings, figurines, and ivories with comparable subjects 
securely dated to the early Late Bronze Age have led to a complete reassessment 
in favour of the Ring of Nestor’s authenticity.", "document_ref": "d5", 
"source_ref": "s5", "qualifiers": ["chronology: early_Late_Bronze_Age", 
"approx_date_range: 16th–15th century BC"], "limitations": ["chronology inferred
