      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Scholarly article hosted on Academia.edu that 
analyzes the iconography of the Ring of Nestor. It situates the ring within the 
broader context of Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates, treating it as 
part of the category of doubtful or questioned artefacts. The study focuses on 
the imagery on the ring and explores how its iconography relates to 
Minoan–Mycenaean religious or funerary themes while engaging with questions 
about whether the object is an authentic ancient artefact or a later forgery.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "Hudler's 2019 academic article on authenticity and 
archaeological provenance of antiquities in the digital age. It discusses cases 
of \"Gemmae dubitandae\"—doubtful gems—including the Ring of Nestor, using them 
to illustrate problems that arise when objects lack secure archaeological 
properties such as documented provenance and excavation context. The article 
emphasizes the role of archaeological documentation in assessing authenticity 
and uses the Ring of Nestor as an example of an artefact whose status and 
provenance have been questioned.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "Arthur J. Evans's Journal of Hellenic Studies 
article, originally published in 1925, which introduces and interprets the Ring 
of Nestor as part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals 
found at Thisbe in Boeotia. Evans gives a detailed description and 
interpretation of the ring's iconography and argues that it offers \"a glimpse 
into the Minoan after-world\", presenting the imagery as reflecting Minoan 
beliefs about the soul and the afterlife. The article frames the treasure as 
funerary grave goods and attributes the ring stylistically to Minoan or 
Minoan-influenced craftsmanship despite its Boeotian context.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "Evans's 1925 monograph or offprint, digitized by 
