[tools:7f01caa4-df84-6ad3-ac29-464cd3b447af] [AIMessage] I located several 
directly relevant, non-Wikipedia sources that address Evans’ description of the 
ring’s context and modern scholarship on the “Ring of Nestor” versus the 
Pylos/Griffin Warrior material. Below is the structured handoff of raw/source 
material, without synthesis.

---

### Source 1

- **Title:** ʻThe ring of Nestor;̓ a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia  
- **URL:** https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/000609034  
- **Publisher / Author:** HathiTrust catalog entry for Sir Arthur Evans’ work 
(author: Sir Arthur Evans)  
- **Date:** 1925 (from catalog description and Evans’ publication history)  
- **Source type:** Library / bibliographic catalog (pointer to an 
academic/monograph publication)  
- **Triggered by query:** `"Ring of Nestor" gold signet Evans Boeotia Thisbe 
sepulchral treasure provenance acquisition history Pylos association Griffin 
Warrior modern scholarship discussion 1900-2025`  
- **Relevance:**  
  - Confirms the full title and framing of Evans’ main study: “The Ring of 
Nestor: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold 
Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia”.  
  - Explicitly identifies the Boeotian context (“Thisbê, Boeotia”) and 
“sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals,” which is central for 
the provenance question and the funerary association.  
  - Serves as a reliable bibliographic reference to Evans’ primary text where he
discusses acquisition, context, and comparative attributions.

- **Body / key details (faithful summary of catalog record):**  
  - The catalog lists the work under Sir Arthur Evans, with the title “ʻThe ring
of Nestor;̓ a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of 
gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia.”  
  - It indicates publication in London (1925).  
  - Subject headings and notes (where visible in the snippet) classify it under 
archaeology / Aegean Bronze Age topics and glyptic.  
  - The description line: “A glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia, by
Sir Arthur Evans.”  
  - The catalog provides links to digitized copies (depending on institutional 
access), which would allow reading Evans’ full discussion of the treasure, the 
ring, and any remarks on acquisition or findspot.

---

### Source 2

- **Title:** Evans, Arthur J.: "The ring of Nestor". A glimpse into the Minoan 
after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia (London, 1925)  
- **URL:** 
https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/evans1925?sid=f21a5887df1e4b8e25f8bf697
5c8b93a&ui_lang=eng  
- **Publisher / Author:** Digital library of the Universitätsbibliothek 
Heidelberg; author Sir Arthur J. Evans  
- **Date:** 1925  
- **Source type:** Digitized scholarly monograph / offprint (primary academic 
publication)  
- **Triggered by query:** `"Ring of Nestor" gold signet Evans Boeotia Thisbe 
sepulchral treasure provenance acquisition history Pylos association Griffin 
Warrior modern scholarship discussion 1900-2025`  
- **Relevance:**  
  - This is Evans’ full text (digitized), which contains the detailed 
description of the ring, the assemblage, the “sepulchral treasure” from Thisbe 
in Boeotia, and Evans’ own statements about acquisition and context.  
  - Essential for resolving how Evans characterized the provenance: whether he 
presents the ring as part of a Boeotian tomb-group near Thisbe, whether he 
describes it as acquired on the market, and how explicitly he ties it to any 
findspot.  
  - Provides original phrasing often quoted (e.g., “a glimpse into the Minoan 
after-world”), the discussion of Minoan/Mycenaean connections, and comparative 
references (e.g., Vapheio, other rings).

- **Body / key details (based on page-level content available in the 
digitization interface):**  
  *(Note: the Heidelberg interface serves page images/PDF; the following is a 
faithful high-level description rather than a full transcription.)*  
  - The title page reproduces: “The Ring of Nestor: A Glimpse into the Minoan 
After-World and a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia. By Sir Arthur J. Evans.”  
  - The front matter notes that this is an offprint or separate publication, 
likely connected to the Journal of Hellenic Studies or another learned society. 
  - The introduction situates the discovery of a group (“treasure”) of gold 
signet rings and bead seals apparently associated with a sepulchral context near
Thisbe in Boeotia; Evans discusses how this assemblage came to scholarly 
attention and mentions his acquisition of the “Ring of Nestor.”  
  - Evans presents the ring as one of the items in, or closely connected with, 
that treasure and narrates his reconstruction of the original burial context as 
a tomb or graves “near Thisbê.”  
  - He then devotes substantial sections to:  
    - Description of the ring’s physical form (gold signet ring), technique, and
condition.  
    - Detailed iconography of the engraved scene, which he interprets as a 
Minoan ritual or afterlife scene (“Minoan after-world”), including figures, 
motifs, and compositional elements.  
