{"query": {"id": "20260628152806090424_1e8cc55a", "handle": "q1", "text": 
"\"Ring of Nestor\" gold ring Pylos Mycenaean description iconography", 
"importance": "core"}, "items": [{"id": "20260628152933722355_fbdf745a", "type":
"Evidence", "text": "Evans presents the Ring of Nestor as part of a sepulchral 
treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia, and describes
it as a large gold signet ring with an elongated elliptical bezel engraved in 
intaglio with a complex composition.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932210323_cd3e7ecc"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628152933778854_9337e2a6", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans emphasizes 
the unusually large dimensions and fine gold work of the Ring of Nestor compared
to typical Minoan and Mycenaean rings, highlighting the precise cutting of 
figures in intaglio and the elaborate composition in several registers on the 
bezel.", "source_ids": ["20260628152932210323_cd3e7ecc"], "citations": [1]}, 
{"id": "20260628152933837144_2828620d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans 
identifies iconographic elements on the Ring of Nestor's bezel including a 
female figure on a boat, altars or platforms, sacred trees, baetyls (sacred 
stones), and multiple female figures engaged in ritual actions within a 
ceremonial landscape.", "source_ids": ["20260628152932210323_cd3e7ecc"], 
"citations": [1]}, {"id": "20260628152933894740_49261050", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Evans interprets the composition on the Ring of Nestor as a glimpse 
into the Minoan after-world, suggesting that the imagery reflects notions of the
soul's voyage and cultic rites, with priestess or goddess figures attending a 
soul's journey.", "source_ids": ["20260628152932210323_cd3e7ecc"], "citations": 
[1]}, {"id": "20260628152933969638_f56be3b7", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Hesperia article by Davis and Stocker reports that the Griffin Warrior tomb near
Tholos Tomb IV at Pylos, dated to Late Helladic IIA, contained four gold signet 
rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes, and that many grave goods in the tomb
were manufactured in the Minoan world.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932232511_2e25e8d6"], "citations": [2]}, {"id": 
"20260628152934046165_919b2594", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In their 
comparative discussion of Minoan ritual imagery on signet rings, Davis and 
Stocker refer to the Ring of Nestor as a classic example of Minoan-style 
ceremonial iconography on a mainland-found gold signet ring, and use it as a key
comparator when interpreting the Griffin Warrior rings.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932232511_2e25e8d6"], "citations": [2]}, {"id": 
"20260628152934115932_80b29152", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Cambridge 
chapter notes that, like the Ring of Nestor, another ring shows a combination of
scenes including a female individual on a boat, altars, sacred trees, and 
baetyls linked to a divine theme.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932253807_1272d186"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628152934188477_be2b9271", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Ring of Nestor
is discussed within a chapter entitled 'Fakes and Dubitanda' in a Cambridge 
University Press volume on Aegean Bronze Age art, placing it among objects whose
authenticity or status is being critically examined.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932253807_1272d186"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628152934252353_5a685e71", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Academia.edu 
paper 'SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE \"RING OF NESTOR\"' is 
described as analyzing the ring's iconography in the context of Aegean forgeries
and authenticity debates, revisiting Evans's interpretation and comparing the 
ring's motifs with those on other Minoan and Mycenaean signet rings.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628152932279017_c6ffcac0"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628152934318781_55b6070a", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Academia.edu-hosted PDF of Davis and Stocker's article provides technical 
descriptions, including dimensions and materials, for four gold signet rings 
from the Griffin Warrior tomb and uses the Ring of Nestor as a comparative 
example within the broader corpus of Minoan-style ritual rings.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932305579_7a149446"], "citations": [5]}, {"id": 
"20260628152934393751_da81b270", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The ResearchGate 
paper on the 5-priestess signet ring describes an elongated elliptical bezel 
bearing multiple priestess figures and analyzes the ring's geometry and possible
astronomical interpretations of its miniature art composition.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932328858_884336fd"], "citations": [6]}, {"id": 
"20260628152934454682_e0b2002d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Past magazine 
article notes that among gold artifacts from the Palace of Nestor area, there is
a gold signet ring from Tholos IV and that the only other gold signet ring known
from this area is the famous Ring of Nestor, purchased by Arthur Evans.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628152932352612_0b2aa3cc"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628153246490942_7ca13c63", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor is
a large gold signet ring featuring an elongated elliptical bezel engraved in 
intaglio, with unusually fine gold work and a complex multi-register composition
compared to typical Minoan and Mycenaean rings.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932210323_cd3e7ecc"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628153246580722_e1e06e92", "type": "Claim", "text": "The bezel of the Ring
of Nestor bears a complex ritual or funerary scene including multiple female 
figures, a female figure on a boat, altars or platforms, sacred trees, and 
baetyls arranged within a ceremonial landscape.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932210323_cd3e7ecc", "20260628152932232511_2e25e8d6", 
"20260628152932253807_1272d186", "20260628152932279017_c6ffcac0"], "citations": 
[1, 2, 3, 4]}, {"id": "20260628153246725622_db4eafae", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Evans interpreted the imagery on the Ring of Nestor as representing the Minoan 
afterworld and the soul’s voyage accompanied by priestess or goddess figures and
cultic rites.", "source_ids": ["20260628152932210323_cd3e7ecc"], "citations": 
[1]}, {"id": "20260628153246786731_0cb49ece", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans 
regarded the Ring of Nestor as manufactured in a Minoan (Cretan) workshop on 
stylistic and craftsmanship grounds, and modern scholars use its imagery as a 
classic example of Minoan-style ceremonial iconography on a gold signet ring 
found on the Greek mainland.", "source_ids": ["20260628152932210323_cd3e7ecc", 
"20260628152932232511_2e25e8d6"], "citations": [1, 2]}, {"id": 
"20260628153246919417_0ede0aab", "type": "Claim", "text": "According to Evans, 
the Ring of Nestor formed part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and
bead seals reportedly found at ancient Thisbê in Boeotia.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932210323_cd3e7ecc"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628153246986215_ad145c27", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor is
commonly cited as one of very few gold signet rings associated with the Palace 
of Nestor at Pylos area and is noted as a famous ring purchased by Arthur Evans,
although it was not part of the Griffin Warrior tomb assemblage.", "source_ids":
["20260628152932232511_2e25e8d6", "20260628152932352612_0b2aa3cc"], "citations":
[2, 7]}, {"id": "20260628153247105912_ec781de7", "type": "Claim", "text": "The 
Ring of Nestor has been placed by modern scholars within debates on Aegean 
Bronze Age authenticity and forgeries, including discussion in a Cambridge 
chapter on fakes and dubitanda and in a dedicated paper reassessing its 
iconography to evaluate its genuineness.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932253807_1272d186", "20260628152932279017_c6ffcac0"], "citations":
[3, 4]}, {"id": "20260628153247206731_e02332f6", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans
associated the Ring of Nestor conceptually with the Homeric figure Nestor, but 
this naming and connection lack inscriptional or epigraphic confirmation.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628152932210323_cd3e7ecc"], "citations": [1]}], "offset": 
0, "limit": 20, "total": 20, "next_offset": null}
[tools:bdbda0ed-c19e-9db2-e7e1-278c953e540f] [get_report_evidence result] 
{"query": {"id": "20260628152806214665_a178d061", "handle": "q2", "text": 
"\"Ring of Nestor\" Mycenaean archaeology scholarly interpretation 
significance", "importance": "core"}, "items": [{"id": 
"20260628153519974183_b8208493", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"该论文被描述为“对‘涅斯托尔之戒’图像学的分析，置于爱琴赝品和真伪争论的语境之中”
。", "source_ids": ["20260628152932279017_c6ffcac0"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628153520051238_0bb12d7e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Hudler 提到“Gemmae
dubitandae，例如‘涅斯托尔之戒’，说明一旦文物的考古属性……”并以此展开对真伪与考古
来源的讨论。", "source_ids": ["20260628153517960963_2425ecf5"], "citations": 
[8]}, {"id": "20260628153520126241_9bf9bcf9", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"埃文斯文章标题为：“The Ring of Nestor: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World 
and A Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, 
Boeotia”。", "source_ids": ["20260628153517990080_d60d930d"], "citations": [9]},
{"id": "20260628153520193302_aef30036", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"单行本目录列出内容结构：I. Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia (pp. 1–42). II. A glimpse into the Minoan 
after-world (pp. 43–75). Plates I–V。", "source_ids": 
["20260628153518017614_7253ea00"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628153520268254_5be56382", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"馆藏元数据关键词包括：“Nestor; Ring; Minoische Kultur; Thísvi; Greece; 
Kakóvatos”。", "source_ids": ["20260628153518017614_7253ea00"], "citations": 
[10]}, {"id": "20260628153520338709_21924d37", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"摘要片段称：“The study shows that the Ring of Nestor possesses 97% gold 
content, indicating high-quality Ancient Aegean craftsmanship comparable to 
notable pieces like the ...”。", "source_ids": 
["20260628153518052974_47a57a13"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628153520405628_a4e64d12", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Nestor 2020 
书目条目指出：“The Ring of Nestor and the Quest for Authenticity. Pp. 223–252 in
F. Blakolmer (ed.), Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean 
Iconography. (Abstract, p. 223 ...)”。", "source_ids": 
["20260628153518108832_3b0e78c3"], "citations": [13]}, {"id": 
"20260628153520476517_7b72ab00", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"宗教研究摘要中出现：“Demeter and Persephone were worshipped at Mycenaean Pylos.
Bibliography. Scholarly Articles & Books ... Ring of Nestor - Goddess 
U-po-jo-po-ti-ni- ...”。", "source_ids": ["20260628153518141428_209640ef"], 
"citations": [14]}, {"id": "20260628153520545959_e058f53a", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "BMCR 书评提到，Gere “describes Evans’s interpretation of certain 
Mycenaean artefacts, such as the ‘Ring of Nestor’, in terms of representations 
of the Minoan soul or ‘psyche’”。", "source_ids": 
["20260628153518168898_6430daab"], "citations": [15]}, {"id": 
"20260628153545676622_26189eae", "type": "Claim", "text": "Arthur Evans 
interpreted the Ring of Nestor, found among a sepulchral treasure of gold signet
rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê in Boeotia, as depicting a glimpse into a 
specifically ‘Minoan after‑world’ and the Minoan soul or psyche.", "source_ids":
["20260628153517990080_d60d930d", "20260628153518017614_7253ea00", 
"20260628153518168898_6430daab"], "citations": [9, 10, 15]}, {"id": 
"20260628153545834211_a4145609", "type": "Claim", "text": "Modern scholarship 
critically reassesses Evans’s symbolic and eschatological reading of the Ring of
Nestor as an expression of the Minoan soul and afterlife, arguing that his 
interpretation blends archaeological evidence with a speculative construct of 
Minoan culture and the after‑world.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153518168898_6430daab"], "citations": [15]}, {"id": 
"20260628153545926030_a1f43234", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans stylistically 
attributed the Ring of Nestor to Minoan craftsmanship or to artisans influenced 
by Minoan culture, even though the ring was reportedly found in mainland Greece 
at Thisbê, Boeotia.", "source_ids": ["20260628153517990080_d60d930d", 
"20260628153518017614_7253ea00"], "citations": [9, 10]}, {"id": 
"20260628153546055868_3a0902ad", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor is
a central case in scholarly debates over authenticity and provenance of Aegean 
glyptic, being classified by some authors (e.g., Hudler) among ‘Gemmae 
dubitandae’ and discussed in terms of uncertain archaeological attributes and 
documentary records.", "source_ids": ["20260628152932279017_c6ffcac0", 
"20260628153517960963_2425ecf5"], "citations": [4, 8]}, {"id": 
"20260628153546198534_24999455", "type": "Claim", "text": "A. G. Vlachopoulos’s 
chapter ‘The Ring of Nestor and the Quest for Authenticity’ re‑examines previous
doubts about the ring’s authenticity by combining metallurgical analysis and 
stylistic comparison, reporting approximately 97% gold content and arguing that 
this high‑purity alloy is comparable to other high‑quality Ancient Aegean 
goldwork.", "source_ids": ["20260628153518052974_47a57a13", 
"20260628153518108832_3b0e78c3"], "citations": [11, 13]}, {"id": 
"20260628153546329564_2e47cc90", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor 
functions as a key object in Aegean iconographic research, with at least one 
study analyzing its imagery while situating the ring within the broader context 
of Aegean forgeries and debates over the authenticity of glyptic and other 
artefacts.", "source_ids": ["20260628152932279017_c6ffcac0"], "citations": [4]},
{"id": "20260628153546404583_74fa44af", "type": "Claim", "text": "In Mycenaean 
religious studies, the Ring of Nestor is cited as comparative iconographic 
material for reconstructing female divinities (possibly agricultural or 
chthonic) and is linked in scholarly discussion to the Linear B goddess 
designated as U‑po‑jo‑po‑ti‑ni‑.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153518141428_209640ef"], "citations": [14]}, {"id": 
"20260628153546481739_1ed32ae6", "type": "Claim", "text": "Technical research on
Mycenaean gold artefacts from Volos and Dimini uses Evans’s publication of the 
Ring of Nestor as a comparative reference for craftsmanship and composition when
analyzing 165 gold objects from four tholos tombs.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153518081030_0ce2d0fa"], "citations": [12]}], "offset": 0, "limit": 
20, "total": 17, "next_offset": null}
[tools:bdbda0ed-c19e-9db2-e7e1-278c953e540f] [get_report_evidence result] 
{"query": {"id": "20260628153619371994_cd4971b7", "handle": "q4", "text": 
"Establish the basic identity, discovery context, dating, and cultural 
attribution of the Ring of Nestor.", "importance": "core"}, "items": [{"id": 
"20260628153734447623_d4509163", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Article title: 
\"The Ring of Nestor: a Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral 
Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbe, Boeotia\".", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153733188028_3f3f42f9"], "citations": [16]}, {"id": 
"20260628153734528738_f6fbf29c", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The title 
explicitly characterizes the assemblage containing the Ring of Nestor as \"a 
sepulchral treasure\" and locates it \"from Thisbe, Boeotia\".", "source_ids": 
["20260628153733188028_3f3f42f9"], "citations": [16]}, {"id": 
"20260628153734601582_fd3acc97", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The phrase \"a 
Glimpse into the Minoan After-World\" in the title signals Evans’ interpretive 
emphasis on the ring’s imagery as related to Minoan afterlife conceptions.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153733188028_3f3f42f9"], "citations": [16]}, {"id": 
"20260628153734675873_c272025e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "JHS article title:
\"‘The Ring of Nestor’: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and A Sepulchral 
Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\".", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153733216377_f3e67d77"], "citations": [17]}, {"id": 
"20260628153734747792_a32f2fe0", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The snippet from 
the JHS article includes the phrase \"ON GOLD SIGNET-RING FROM BOEOTIA\" and 
refers to comparative material such as \"a chalcedony lentoid from Vapheio\", 
indicating that the Ring of Nestor is analyzed as a Boeotian gold signet ring 
within a comparative glyptic study.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153733216377_f3e67d77"], "citations": [17]}, {"id": 
"20260628153734821411_658e5bd2", "type": "Evidence", "text": "HathiTrust catalog
entry records the work’s title as \"‘The ring of Nestor;’ a glimpse into the 
Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\".", "source_ids": 
["20260628153733248911_ff3b5748"], "citations": [18]}, {"id": 
"20260628153734893535_c3a1c293", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The HathiTrust 
record lists Arthur Evans as the author of the work on the Ring of Nestor 
related to sepulchral gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628153733248911_ff3b5748"], "citations": [18]}, {"id": 
"20260628153734966971_fcefd14b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Snippet from \"The
Lord of the Gold Rings\": \"Both the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos have 
bands strikingly similar ... What does the discovery of the gold rings suggest 
about Minoan influence in ...\".", "source_ids": 
["20260628153733277143_41c38309"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628153735042979_f23abf50", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The quoted phrase 
\"bands strikingly similar\" indicates that the author identifies notable formal
similarity between the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos.", "source_ids": 
["20260628153733277143_41c38309"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628153735115184_0e12cf59", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Snippet from The 
Past article: \"... gold signet ring (from Tholos IV); the only other from this 
area was the famous Ring of Nestor – purchased by Arthur Evans, famous for his 
...\".", "source_ids": ["20260628152932352612_0b2aa3cc"], "citations": [7]}, 
{"id": "20260628153735189726_1ed77983", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The phrase 
\"purchased by Arthur Evans\" in the Griffin Warrior article explicitly states 
that Evans acquired the Ring of Nestor by purchase.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152932352612_0b2aa3cc"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628153735261379_b553fca0", "type": "Evidence", "text": "By describing the 
gold signet ring from Tholos IV at Pylos and noting that \"the only other from 
this area was the famous Ring of Nestor\", the article associates the Ring of 
Nestor with the Pylos region’s corpus of Mycenaean gold signet rings.", 
