───────────────────────────────── Final Report ─────────────────────────────────
                                Research Report                                 

"Ring of Nestor" gold ring Pylos Mycenaean description iconography              

 • Evans presents the Ring of Nestor as part of a sepulchral treasure of gold   
   signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia, and describes it as a large
   gold signet ring with an elongated elliptical bezel engraved in intaglio with
   a complex composition. [1]                                                   
 • Evans emphasizes the unusually large dimensions and fine gold work of the    
   Ring of Nestor compared to typical Minoan and Mycenaean rings, highlighting  
   the precise cutting of figures in intaglio and the elaborate composition in  
   several registers on the bezel. [1]                                          
 • Evans identifies iconographic elements on the Ring of Nestor's bezel         
   including a female figure on a boat, altars or platforms, sacred trees,      
   baetyls (sacred stones), and multiple female figures engaged in ritual       
   actions within a ceremonial landscape. [1]                                   
 • Evans interprets the composition on the Ring of Nestor as a glimpse into the 
   Minoan after-world, suggesting that the imagery reflects notions of the      
   soul's voyage and cultic rites, with priestess or goddess figures attending a
   soul's journey. [1]                                                          
 • The Hesperia article by Davis and Stocker reports that the Griffin Warrior   
   tomb near Tholos Tomb IV at Pylos, dated to Late Helladic IIA, contained four
   gold signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes, and that many grave   
   goods in the tomb were manufactured in the Minoan world. [2]                 
 • In their comparative discussion of Minoan ritual imagery on signet rings,    
   Davis and Stocker refer to the Ring of Nestor as a classic example of        
   Minoan-style ceremonial iconography on a mainland-found gold signet ring, and
   use it as a key comparator when interpreting the Griffin Warrior rings. [2]  
 • The Cambridge chapter notes that, like the Ring of Nestor, another ring shows
   a combination of scenes including a female individual on a boat, altars,     
   sacred trees, and baetyls linked to a divine theme. [3]                      
 • The Ring of Nestor is discussed within a chapter entitled 'Fakes and         
   Dubitanda' in a Cambridge University Press volume on Aegean Bronze Age art,  
   placing it among objects whose authenticity or status is being critically    
   examined. [3]                                                                
 • The Academia.edu paper 'SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE "RING OF 
   NESTOR"' is described as analyzing the ring's iconography in the context of  
   Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates, revisiting Evans's interpretation 
   and comparing the ring's motifs with those on other Minoan and Mycenaean     
   signet rings. [4]                                                            
 • The Academia.edu-hosted PDF of Davis and Stocker's article provides technical
   descriptions, including dimensions and materials, for four gold signet rings 
   from the Griffin Warrior tomb and uses the Ring of Nestor as a comparative   
   example within the broader corpus of Minoan-style ritual rings. [5]          
 • The ResearchGate paper on the 5-priestess signet ring describes an elongated 
   elliptical bezel bearing multiple priestess figures and analyzes the ring's  
   geometry and possible astronomical interpretations of its miniature art      
   composition. [6]                                                             
 • The Past magazine article notes that among gold artifacts from the Palace of 
   Nestor area, there is a gold signet ring from Tholos IV and that the only    
   other gold signet ring known from this area is the famous Ring of Nestor,    
   purchased by Arthur Evans. [7]                                               
 • The Ring of Nestor is a large gold signet ring featuring an elongated        
   elliptical bezel engraved in intaglio, with unusually fine gold work and a   
   complex multi-register composition compared to typical Minoan and Mycenaean  
   rings. [1]                                                                   
 • The bezel of the Ring of Nestor bears a complex ritual or funerary scene     
   including multiple female figures, a female figure on a boat, altars or      
   platforms, sacred trees, and baetyls arranged within a ceremonial landscape. 
   [1][2][3][4]                                                                 
 • Evans interpreted the imagery on the Ring of Nestor as representing the      
   Minoan afterworld and the soul’s voyage accompanied by priestess or goddess  
   figures and cultic rites. [1]                                                
 • Evans regarded the Ring of Nestor as manufactured in a Minoan (Cretan)       
   workshop on stylistic and craftsmanship grounds, and modern scholars use its 
   imagery as a classic example of Minoan-style ceremonial iconography on a gold
   signet ring found on the Greek mainland. [1][2]                              
 • According to Evans, the Ring of Nestor formed part of a sepulchral treasure  
   of gold signet rings and bead seals reportedly found at ancient Thisbê in    
   Boeotia. [1]                                                                 
 • The Ring of Nestor is commonly cited as one of very few gold signet rings    
   associated with the Palace of Nestor at Pylos area and is noted as a famous  
   ring purchased by Arthur Evans, although it was not part of the Griffin      
   Warrior tomb assemblage. [2][7]                                              
 • The Ring of Nestor has been placed by modern scholars within debates on      
   Aegean Bronze Age authenticity and forgeries, including discussion in a      
   Cambridge chapter on fakes and dubitanda and in a dedicated paper reassessing
   its iconography to evaluate its genuineness. [3][4]                          
 • Evans associated the Ring of Nestor conceptually with the Homeric figure     
   Nestor, but this naming and connection lack inscriptional or epigraphic      
   confirmation. [1]                                                            

