 • Arthur Evans interpreted the Ring of Nestor, found among a sepulchral        
   treasure of gold signet rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê in Boeotia, as      
   depicting a glimpse into a specifically ‘Minoan after‑world’ and the Minoan  
   soul or psyche. [9][10][14]                                                  
 • Modern scholarship critically reassesses Evans’s symbolic and eschatological 
   reading of the Ring of Nestor as an expression of the Minoan soul and        
   afterlife, arguing that his interpretation blends archaeological evidence    
   with a speculative construct of Minoan culture and the after‑world. [14]     
 • Evans stylistically attributed the Ring of Nestor to Minoan craftsmanship or 
   to artisans influenced by Minoan culture, even though the ring was reportedly
   found in mainland Greece at Thisbê, Boeotia. [9][10]                         
 • The Ring of Nestor is a central case in scholarly debates over authenticity  
   and provenance of Aegean glyptic, being classified by some authors (e.g.,    
   Hudler) among ‘Gemmae dubitandae’ and discussed in terms of uncertain        
   archaeological attributes and documentary records. [4][8]                    
 • A. G. Vlachopoulos’s chapter ‘The Ring of Nestor and the Quest for           
   Authenticity’ re‑examines previous doubts about the ring’s authenticity by   
   combining metallurgical analysis and stylistic comparison, reporting         
   approximately 97% gold content and arguing that this high‑purity alloy is    
   comparable to other high‑quality Ancient Aegean goldwork. [11][12]           
 • The Ring of Nestor functions as a key object in Aegean iconographic research,
   with at least one study analyzing its imagery while situating the ring within
   the broader context of Aegean forgeries and debates over the authenticity of 
   glyptic and other artefacts. [4]                                             
 • In Mycenaean religious studies, the Ring of Nestor is cited as comparative   
   iconographic material for reconstructing female divinities (possibly         
   agricultural or chthonic) and is linked in scholarly discussion to the Linear
   B goddess designated as U‑po‑jo‑po‑ti‑ni‑. [13]                              
 • Technical research on Mycenaean gold artefacts from Volos and Dimini uses    
   Evans’s publication of the Ring of Nestor as a comparative reference for     
   craftsmanship and composition when analyzing 165 gold objects from four      
   tholos tombs. [15]                                                           

