 • Arthur Evans’s 1925 study presents the Ring of Nestor as belonging to, or    
   closely associated with, a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead 
   seals reportedly originating from a tomb or funerary context near Thisbe in  
   Boeotia, though Evans acknowledges that the assemblage reached him through   
   acquisition rather than controlled excavation and therefore its precise      
   original findspot is uncertain. [18][20]                                     
 • Modern catalog and bibliographic descriptions of Arthur Evans’s work         
   consistently characterize the Ring of Nestor as associated with a sepulchral 
   treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbe in Boeotia, aligning
   with Evans’s own Boeotian attribution. [18][20]                              
 • Modern scholarship and institutional presentations of the Griffin Warrior    
   tomb at Pylos consistently treat its four gold signet rings as securely      
   excavated grave goods from a Late Helladic IIA shaft tomb near the Palace of 
   Nestor, without identifying any of these rings as the Ring of Nestor or      
   suggesting that they share the Boeotian provenance claimed for Evans’s ring. 
   [2][5][21][22][23]                                                           
 • Comparative discussions by Davis, Stocker, and subsequent popular coverage   
   juxtapose the Griffin Warrior’s Pylos rings with other Minoan and Mycenaean  
   rings, including those discussed by Evans, solely to explore stylistic and   
   iconographic parallels, and not as evidence that these objects share a common
   findspot or provenance. [2][5][20]                                           
 • Given the available documentation, the best-supported interpretation is that 
   the Ring of Nestor is an unexcavated artifact attributed by Arthur Evans to a
   Boeotian sepulchral treasure from near Thisbe, whereas the Griffin Warrior’s 
   four gold rings have a secure, excavated provenance in a Late Helladic IIA   
   shaft tomb at Pylos; modern scholarship treats any linkage between these     
   rings as purely comparative and intellectual rather than as a shared         
   provenience claim. [2][5][18][20][21][22]                                    

