   [29]                                                                         
 • The SciSpace index entry lists the title "The ring of Nestor: a glimpse into 
   the Minoan after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and    
   bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia" and attributes it to Arthur Evans. [30]     
 • The brief description on SciSpace restates that the work deals with a gold   
   signet ring called the "Ring of Nestor" and a sepulchral treasure of gold    
   signet rings and bead-seals from Thisbe in Boeotia. [30]                     
 • Arthur Evans’s 1925 booklet treats a gold signet ring he calls the “Ring of  
   Nestor” as part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead‑seals 
   reported to come from Thisbe in Boeotia, and he describes this treasure as   
   grave goods from a Boeotian burial. [18][20][27][30]                         
 • Evans explains that he learned of and acquired the Ring of Nestor and the    
   associated Boeotian sepulchral treasure via intermediaries in the antiquities
   market, and reports ‘Thisbe in Boeotia’ as the findspot based on information 
   given at the time of purchase. [20][27]                                      
 • Evans situates the Ring of Nestor within the wider corpus of Aegean Bronze   
   Age glyptic by comparing it stylistically and iconographically with Minoan   
   and Mycenaean gold signet rings from Crete and mainland Greece, including    
   material associated with Pylos, but he presents these as comparative         
   analogies rather than as evidence that the ring itself was found at Pylos.   
   [20][27]                                                                     
 • Modern bibliographical and digital‑library treatments (HathiTrust,           
   Heidelberg, SciSpace, Aegeus) uniformly describe Evans’s study as dealing    
   with the Ring of Nestor within a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and
   bead‑seals from Thisbe in Boeotia, implicitly maintaining the Boeotian tomb  
   context rather than reassigning the ring to a Pylos provenance.              
   [18][27][28][30]                                                             
 • The Pylos‑focused exhibition and corpus descriptions for Mycenaean gold      
   signet rings discuss rich grave goods from the Pylos region but do not       
   mention the Ring of Nestor, indicating that current Pylos‑region catalogues  
   do not treat this ring as part of the documented Pylian assemblages. [26]    
 • The UC Classics Nestor database functions as a bibliographical tool for      
   Aegean studies, Homeric society, and Indo‑European linguistics and does not  
   itself provide detailed information on the findspot or acquisition history of
   the Ring of Nestor. [29]                                                     
 • At least one modern paper, listed as ‘Some observations on the iconography of
   the “ring of nestor”’, analyses the ring’s imagery within wider debates on   
   Aegean forgeries and authenticity and likely addresses its collection history
   and Evans’s claim of a Thisbe, Boeotia origin, though its detailed arguments 
   and conclusions remain unverified in the present evidence set. [4]           
 • Overall, on the basis of Evans’s narrative and its transmission in modern    
   bibliographic sources, the best‑supported working provenance for the Ring of 
   Nestor is as a market‑acquired object said to belong to a sepulchral treasure
   of gold signet rings and bead‑seals from a Boeotian tomb at or near Thisbe,  
   while associations with Pylos function as literary and comparative frames    
   rather than documented findspot claims; nonetheless, the reliance on         
   antiquities‑market information means that the Thisbe attribution cannot be   
   regarded as securely verified. [20][27][28]                                  

